---
// src/pages/about.astro
import ContentLayout from '../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
---

<ContentLayout title="About | Curiosity Engine">
  <section class="about-container">
    <!-- Left Side: Main Title -->
    <h1>About Me</h1>
  </section>

  <!-- Main Narrative Text (Your Written Copy) -->
  <article class="main-about">
    <p>
      Long before I started writing automated tests for software, I was using
      code and computational statistics to analyze the contested authorship of
      16th-century plays.
    </p>

    <p>
      While studying English and English Language Joint Honours at De Montfort
      University in Leicester, I learned that language could be analyzed
      computationally. In contrast to traditional literary studies, language was
      stripped of subjective human interpretation and mapped through data. Long
      before most people encountered Natural Language Processing through modern
      AI chatbots, I knew it as a statistical tool that revealed the underlying
      patterns in human expression.
    </p>
    <h2>The Academic Footprint</h2>
    <p>
      That fascination led me to pursue a master’s degree in Linguistics and
      Communication in the Netherlands, following a data science-oriented track
      that combined language, statistics, programming, and computational
      analysis.
    </p>
    <p>
      Along the way, I had the opportunity to contribute as a co-author to a
      published psychology research paper. Looking back, that experience taught
      me something that still influences how I work today: good answers rarely
      come from assumptions. They come from asking questions, collecting
      evidence, and following where the data leads.
    </p>
    <h2>Testing? Testing?</h2>
    <p>
      When I stumbled into Quality Assurance afterwards, I realized something
      fundamental: code, systems, and test architectures are just another form
      of language.
    </p>
    <p>
      Just like human language, systems require interpretation. They rely on
      strict rules of syntax, boundaries of meaning, and underlying structure. A
      broken automated test suite or a fragile system locator isn't just a
      technical glitch; it's a breakdown in communication.
    </p>
    <p>
      Although my career eventually moved away from academia, I never left that
      mindset behind.
    </p>
    <h2>Welcome to Curiosity Engine</h2>
    <p>
      Welcome to my corner of the internet. The Curiosity Engine has been
      running for quite some time now and this website reflects what I'm
      learning, what I'm building, and what I'm currently curious about.
      Sometimes that's quality engineering or test automation. Sometimes it's a
      homelab project, a crochet pattern, or a completely unrelated rabbit hole
      that seemed too interesting not to explore.
    </p>
    <p>The subject changes. The curiosity does not.</p>
  </article>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .about-container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between; /* Pushes text left and links right */
    align-items: baseline; /* snaps items to the same height */
    flex-wrap: wrap; /* Allows smooth wrapping on small mobile screens */
    gap: 1rem; /* Keeps items from touching on mid-sized screens */
  }

  .cv-tagpill {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.35rem;

    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    border-radius: 9999px;
    text-decoration: none;
    text-align: justify;

    /* Light Mode defaults */
    background-color: var(--active-color);
    color: var(--bg-color);
    border: 1px solid var(--active-color);

    transition: all 0.2s ease;
  }

  .cv-tagpill:hover {
    transform: translateY(-1px);
    background-color: var(--accent-color);
    opacity: 0.9;
  }

  .dl-icon {
    width: 0.95rem;
    height: 0.95rem;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }
</style>
